{
  "dom_id": "step-q66348",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $g(x)=2x-1,$ find  $(fg)(3).$",
  "worked_solution": "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(3),$ we find $f(3)$ and $g(3),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(3)$ and $g(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =(3)^{2}+1=10 \\\\ g(3) & =2(3)-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(3)=f(3)g(3)=10\\cdot 5=50\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)(2x-1) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(3) & =2(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}+2(3)-1 \\\\  & =54-9+6-1 \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$",
